# Action Authorization Grants and Replay Safety

An action plan is not permission to mutate. Authorization is phase-bound,
identity-bound, short-lived, and consumed once. This workbook exercises that
lifecycle without a database.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_verification!()
```

## 1) Plan first, authorize later

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions

assert {:ok, plan} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(
  Selecto.Domain.Examples.work_items(),
  %{action: :archive, target: 7}
)
assert %SelectoUpdato.PlannedOperation{} = plan.operation_builder

resolver = fn request, context, _opts ->
  if request.phase == :execute and context.actor.id == 17, do: :enabled, else: :hidden
end
context = %{actor: %{id: 17}, tenant: %{tenant_id: 101}}

assert {:ok, authorized, decision, grant} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.authorize_and_issue(
    plan, :execute, context, capability_resolver: resolver
  )
assert decision.status == :enabled
assert is_reference(grant)
```

## 2) Consume exactly once

The adapter receives this evidence in its context. Passing it through unchanged
lets command compilation consume the grant. Here we call the grant boundary
directly to isolate replay behavior from a database adapter.

```elixir
assert {:ok, authorization_context} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.authorization_context(context)
evidence = %{
  action: authorized.action,
  capability: authorized.capability,
  operation: authorized.operation_builder.type,
  operation_fingerprint:
    SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.operation_fingerprint(authorized.operation_builder),
  phase: :execute,
  authorization_context: authorization_context
}

assert :ok = SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.consume(grant, evidence)
assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{
  type: :authorization,
  details: %{code: :unregistered_action_authorization}
}} = SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.consume(grant, evidence)
```

## 3) Bind the grant to the immutable plan

Changing the target on the opaque pre-authorization plan is rejected before an
adapter can receive it.

```elixir
defmodule AuthorizationProbeAdapter do
  def execute_action(_plan, _context, _opts), do: {:ok, :unexpected_execution}
end

altered = %{plan | target: %{"id" => 99}}
assert {:error, {:validation_error, _message, %{code: :action_policy_mismatch}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
    AuthorizationProbeAdapter, altered, context, capability_resolver: resolver
  )
```

## 4) Expire unused permission

The tiny TTL is only for this deterministic example. Production values should
cover the expected request hop while remaining bounded.

```elixir
assert {:ok, _authorized3, _decision3, grant3} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.authorize_and_issue(
    plan, :execute, context,
    capability_resolver: resolver,
    action_authorization_grant_ttl: 5
  )
Process.sleep(25)

assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{
  type: :authorization,
  details: %{code: :unregistered_action_authorization}
}} = SelectoUpdato.ActionAuthorization.consume(grant3, %{
  action: plan.action, capability: plan.capability,
  operation: :update, phase: :execute
})
```

Preview and execute require separate authorization calls. Never serialize the
opaque plan handle or grant into a durable job or browser-owned token.
See [domain actions](selecto_actions_workbook.livemd) for capability decisions
and [guarded execution](selecto_action_execution_workbook.livemd) for consumption
inside a real write.
